{
  "id": "scattering",
  "model": "mobile-robots-in-the-plane",
  "name": "Scattering",
  "origin": {
    "ref": "Dieudonne2009Scatter",
    "year": 2009,
    "note": "Dieudonné and Petit showed scattering is deterministically impossible and gave a randomized solution (preprint 2007)."
  },
  "related": [
    {
      "problem": "gathering",
      "relation": "Scattering is the opposite task: gathering creates a multiplicity point, scattering removes all of them. Pattern formation from configurations with multiplicities needs scattering first [Bramas2016Probabilistic]."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Robots that may start at shared points must move to pairwise distinct points.",
  "statement": "$n$ robots start at points of the plane, several possibly at the same point (a **multiplicity**). They must reach a configuration in which all positions are distinct, and stay there.",
  "primer": {
    "intro": "Imagine a box of marbles that were all tipped onto the same spot of a table, and you want them to roll apart so that no two touch the same point. In the scattering problem the marbles are $n$ **robots**: they start in the plane, several possibly at the same point (a **multiplicity**), and must reach a **configuration** (the positions of all robots) in which all positions are distinct, and then stay distinct. Robots are **oblivious** (they forget everything after each move) and work in **Look–Compute–Move cycles**: take a **snapshot** of everyone, compute a destination, move. In each **round** an **adversary** (an opponent who controls everything the algorithm does not) chooses which robots act, and each of them performs one full cycle; this is the SSYNC **scheduler**. In the basic catalogued version robots may flip coins (**randomized**), and a robot can see how many robots share each point (**strong multiplicity detection**).",
    "ideas": [
      {
        "kind": "impossibility",
        "title": "Two robots on one spot can never be told apart",
        "variant": "ssync-randomized",
        "ref": "Dieudonne2009Scatter",
        "idea": "Start with all robots on the same point, give them all the same **local coordinate system** (the axes, unit and handedness in which a robot reads its snapshot), and let the adversary activate all of them in every round. Each robot sees exactly the same snapshot, so a **deterministic** rule (one without coin flips) sends every robot to the same destination. After the move they are again all on one point, with identical snapshots, and so on forever.",
        "analysis": "By induction, there is an execution in which the robots never separate, so no deterministic algorithm solves scattering [Dieudonne2009Scatter]. The argument does not care whether robots remember the past or can count robots at a point: the robots' memories are equal as well, so their histories never differ. Only randomness can make two robots on the same point behave differently.",
        "why_it_matters": "It is the cleanest example of why some robot problems need randomness at all, and it explains why scattering is studied only with coin flips."
      },
      {
        "kind": "algorithm",
        "title": "Flip a coin, then move inside your own Voronoi cell",
        "variant": "ssync-randomized",
        "ref": "Dieudonne2009Scatter",
        "idea": "Each time a robot is activated:\n1. Look at the occupied points and compute the *Voronoi diagram*: the **Voronoi cell** of an occupied point is the set of places strictly closer to it than to any other occupied point.\n2. Flip a fair coin.\n3. Heads: move to some point of your own cell other than where you stand. Tails: stay.",
        "analysis": "Robots on different points never collide again, because each moves only inside its own cell and the cells do not overlap. Two robots on the same point are separated in a round where exactly one of them moves; if both are active this happens with probability $1/2$, so with probability 1 they eventually separate [Dieudonne2009Scatter]. If all movers from a point pick the same destination, a crowd of $q$ robots is at best split in two per round, so about $\\log_2 q$ rounds are needed; robots that can count the robots on their point scatter in $O(1)$ expected rounds [Clement2010Cost], and with weaker counting $O(\\log\\log n)$ rounds, which is optimal for algorithms that use the fewest random bits [Bramas2017RandomBit].",
        "why_it_matters": "Moving only inside your own Voronoi cell is a reusable trick for keeping already separated robots apart, and randomized scattering is used to break symmetry before running deterministic algorithms such as pattern formation."
      }
    ]
  },
  "glossary": [
    {
      "term": "robot",
      "definition": "a point of the plane that repeatedly observes the other robots and moves; all robots are identical and run the same algorithm",
      "avoid": [
        "agent"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "multiplicity",
      "definition": "a point of the plane occupied by several robots",
      "avoid": []
    },
    {
      "term": "configuration",
      "definition": "the positions of all robots at one time (a multiset of points when several robots may share one)",
      "avoid": []
    },
    {
      "term": "oblivious",
      "definition": "a robot keeps no memory between cycles: each Compute depends only on the current snapshot",
      "avoid": []
    },
    {
      "term": "Look–Compute–Move cycle",
      "definition": "the repeated behaviour of a robot: Look (take a snapshot), Compute (a destination point from the snapshot), Move (towards that destination)",
      "avoid": [
        "Look-Compute-Move"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "snapshot",
      "definition": "what a Look returns: the positions of the robots that the robot sees, expressed in its local coordinate system",
      "avoid": []
    },
    {
      "term": "round",
      "definition": "in FSYNC and SSYNC, one step of time in which every activated robot performs one full Look–Compute–Move cycle",
      "avoid": []
    },
    {
      "term": "adversary",
      "definition": "an imagined opponent that makes every choice the algorithm does not control: activations, the duration of phases, where non-rigid moves stop, and the local coordinate systems",
      "avoid": []
    },
    {
      "term": "scheduler",
      "definition": "the rule, controlled by the adversary, that decides the activation of robots and the duration of their phases: FSYNC, SSYNC or ASYNC as defined in the model",
      "avoid": []
    },
    {
      "term": "randomized",
      "definition": "robots may use random bits (coin flips) in Compute; guarantees hold with probability 1 or in expectation",
      "avoid": []
    },
    {
      "term": "strong multiplicity detection",
      "definition": "a snapshot gives, for every occupied point, the exact number of robots on it",
      "avoid": []
    },
    {
      "term": "deterministic",
      "definition": "robots use no random bits: the destination is a function of the snapshot",
      "avoid": []
    },
    {
      "term": "Voronoi cell",
      "definition": "for an occupied point $p$, the set of places of the plane strictly closer to $p$ than to any other occupied point",
      "avoid": []
    },
    {
      "term": "local coordinate system",
      "definition": "each robot's own origin (its position), axes, unit of length and handedness, in which it reads its snapshot and computes its destination",
      "avoid": []
    }
  ],
  "history": "Scattering was first a **feasibility** question: it cannot be solved deterministically, so randomness is needed [Dieudonne2009Scatter]. The measures then became **expected rounds and random bits** [Clement2010Cost] [Bramas2017RandomBit], and keeping the swarm connected under limited visibility [Izumi2018Connectivity].",
  "measures": [
    {
      "id": "time",
      "name": "Time",
      "description": "rounds (FSYNC, SSYNC) or epochs (ASYNC) until the task is done; \"finite\" when only solvability is known"
    },
    {
      "id": "colours",
      "name": "Colours",
      "description": "colours of the light per robot (0 for oblivious robots)"
    }
  ],
